From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kaya Kaan Tuna <kayatuna@forecr.io>
Date: Tue, 18 Aug 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] waylandsink: never use the udmabuf pool for the ALLOCATION
 response

gst_wayland_sink_propose_allocation() creates a GstVideoDmabufPool via
gst_video_dmabuf_pool_new() whenever a udmabuf allocator is available
on the system (GST_IS_UDMABUF_ALLOCATOR(allocator)), instead of the
older/simpler wl_shm-backed GstWlVideoBufferPool every prior GStreamer
release used unconditionally. This is new in this exact 1.28 release
(gst-libs/gst/video/gstvideodmabufpool.c carries "Since: 1.28" and a
2025 copyright).

This turned out to be a two-layer problem on real Jetson Thor hardware
(DSBOARD-THRMAX, GStreamer 1.28.2), diagnosed against a pipeline that
feeds NVIDIA's proprietary, closed-source nvvideoconvert into
waylandsink:

 1. As originally written, the function never configures the pool at
    all before handing it upstream via the ALLOCATION query response,
    so gst_buffer_pool_set_active() later fails outright with "pool
    was not configured" and the pipeline never reaches PLAYING:

      gstbufferpool.c:555:gst_buffer_pool_set_active:<videodmabufpool0>
      pool was not configured
      ERROR: from element .../Gstnvvideoconvert:nvvideoconvert0:
      failed to activate bufferpool

    Removing /dev/udmabuf access (so GST_IS_UDMABUF_ALLOCATOR is never
    true and this branch is never taken) made the exact same pipeline
    preroll and play cleanly, isolating the bug to this branch.

 2. Actually configuring GstVideoDmabufPool correctly (its own
    set_config() hard-requires GST_BUFFER_POOL_OPTION_VIDEO_META and
    GST_BUFFER_POOL_OPTION_VIDEO_ALIGNMENT to be present, and always
    rejects+self-corrects the first config it's given per its own
    UDMABUF_ALIGNMENT_MASK contract, so the caller has to retry once)
    gets the pipeline running, but produces visibly corrupted output:
    torn/shifted horizontal banding across the whole frame. The pool
    unconditionally forces every plane's row stride up to a 256-byte
    (UDMABUF_ALIGNMENT_MASK) boundary as part of that same set_config()
    logic. nvvideoconvert has no idea this pool type or its alignment
    contract exists -- it writes pixel rows using its own, unpadded
    stride, while the buffer's GstVideoMeta now advertises the pool's
    256-aligned one. The compositor reads each row from the wrong
    offset against what was actually written, which is exactly this
    kind of banding. There's no way to fix this from the waylandsink
    side alone -- it would require nvvideoconvert itself (proprietary,
    not something this layer can patch) to honour the negotiated
    alignment.

Since the hardware-accelerated-source case this layer cares about
can't safely use the new pool type at all, stop choosing it based on
allocator type and always take the wl_shm-backed
GstWlVideoBufferPool path instead -- exactly what every GStreamer
release before 1.28 did unconditionally, and confirmed tear-free on
this hardware earlier in the same diagnosis. GstWlVideoBufferPool's
own config handling isn't specific to shm allocators -- it's handed
whatever allocator gst_udmabuf_allocator_get()/gst_shm_allocator_get()
picked either way, both before and after this change; only the pool
*class* selection is affected.

Upstream-Status: Pending
Signed-off-by: Kaya Kaan Tuna <kayatuna@forecr.io>
---
 ext/wayland/gstwaylandsink.c | 4 +---
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/ext/wayland/gstwaylandsink.c b/ext/wayland/gstwaylandsink.c
index 1111111..2222222 100644
--- a/ext/wayland/gstwaylandsink.c
+++ b/ext/wayland/gstwaylandsink.c
@@ -896,9 +896,7 @@ gst_wayland_sink_propose_allocation (GstBaseSink * bsink, GstQuery * query)
   if (need_pool && !gst_video_is_dma_drm_caps (caps)) {
     GstStructure *config;

-    if (GST_IS_UDMABUF_ALLOCATOR (allocator)) {
-      pool = gst_video_dmabuf_pool_new ();
-    } else {
+    {
       pool = gst_wl_video_buffer_pool_new ();
       config = gst_buffer_pool_get_config (pool);
       gst_buffer_pool_config_set_params (config, caps, size, 2, 0);
--
2.43.0
